  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1024 AM MST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nPleasant seasonal weather expected on this Thanksgiving day. Friday \nlooks just as pleasant. Temperatures will increase slightly above \nseasonal normals by Saturday before winds pick up over the evening \nhours. Those winds are predominantly drawn out of the northwest as a \npacific system impacts our neighboring states to the north. No rain \nchances for us this weekend, but that temperature advection will \ncool our area down going into the beginning of next week. Shortwaves \nin that trough pattern will generate sustained wind speeds up to \n20-25 mph out of the west with the greatest gusts impacting \neastern mountain slopes. The impression of a ridge follows \nbringing lighter winds and temperatures bump back up over the \nbeginning of next week, but some shortwaves in the trailing zonal \npattern may create more breezy conditions Monday evening.\n\nA distinct trough sets up on Wednesday, and model agreement \nincreases confidence in another chance for precipitation arriving \nthat evening. This Pacific system is expected to cool temperatures \ndown, and PWs approach 0.75\". Snow levels drop that evening, so snow \nchances in the area mountains cannot be ruled out. The aftermath of \nthis storm is uncertain as some models allude to a backdoor flow out \nof the northeast late next week, others suggest a northwesterly flow \ncustomary with a pacific ridge following a trough. Either way, \nanticipate a cooler start to the beginning of December.",
